  1→# Joy of VEX: Trigonometry & Oscillation
  2→
  3→> Tutorial examples from The Joy of VEX video series by Matt Estela.
  4→> Source: https://www.youtube.com/@MattEstela
  5→
  6→## Quick Reference
  7→```vex
  8→@P.y = sin(@P.x);  // Sine Wave Output Range
  9→float d = length(@P);  // Length Function and Distance Calculations
 10→v@up = set(sin(@Time), 0, cos(@Time));  // Rotating Up Vector with Time
 11→```
 12→
 13→## Trigonometric Functions
 14→
 15→### Compound assignment operators [Needs Review] [[Ep1, 100:42](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9gB1zBa9Lg4&t=6042s)]
 16→```vex
 17→vector pos = @P * chv('fancyscale');
 18→vector center = chv('center');
 19→float delta = distance(pos, center);
 20→@P *= ch('scaling');
 21→@Cd = fit(sin(delta), -1, 1, 0, 1);
 22→```
 23→Demonstrates compound assignment operators in VEX, specifically the *= operator which multiplies and assigns in one step (cleaner than writing @P = @P * ch('scaling')). The code shows how compound operators work with geometry attributes and channel references, making code more concise and readable.
 24→
 25→### Sine Function for Color Animation [[Ep1, 44:12](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9gB1zBa9Lg4&t=2652s)]
 26→```vex
 27→@Cd = float(@ptnum)/ch('scale');
 28→
 29→@Cd = sin(@ptnum);
 30→
 31→@Cd = sin(@ptnum/100);
 32→
 33→@Cd = sin(float(@ptnum)/100);
 34→
 35→@Cd = sin(float(@ptnum)*ch('scale'));
 36→
 37→float foo = float(@ptnum)/ch('scale');
 38→@Cd = sin(foo);
 39→```
 40→Demonstrates using the sine function to create oscillating color values based on point numbers. Shows progression from basic division to scaling point numbers appropriately (dividing by 100 or using a channel reference) before applying sine, and introduces storing intermediate calculations in variables for clarity.
 41→
 42→### Sine Function for Color Animation [[Ep1, 44:16](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9gB1zBa9Lg4&t=2656s)]
 43→```vex
 44→@Cd = float(@ptnum)/ch('scale');
 45→
 46→@Cd = sin(@ptnum);
 47→
 48→@Cd = sin(@ptnum/100);
 49→
 50→@Cd = sin(float(@ptnum)/100);
 51→
 52→@Cd = sin(float(@ptnum)*ch('scale'));
 53→
 54→float foo = float(@ptnum)*ch('scale');
 55→@Cd = sin(foo);
 56→```
 57→Demonstrates the progressive refinement of using the sine function with point numbers to create animated color patterns. Shows the importance of scaling point numbers to reasonable ranges (dividing by 100 or using channel references) to work effectively with trigonometric functions, and introduces storing intermediate calculations in variables.
 58→
 59→## Waves & Oscillation
 60→
 61→### Sine Wave with Integer Division [[Ep1, 47:12](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9gB1zBa9Lg4&t=2832s)]
 62→```vex
 63→@Cd = sin(@ptnum/100);
 64→
 65→@Cd = sin(@ptnum);
 66→
 67→@Cd = sin(@ptnum/100);
 68→
 69→@Cd = sin(float(@ptnum)/100);
 70→
 71→@Cd = sin(float(@ptnum)*ch('scale'));
 72→
 73→float foo = float(@ptnum)/ch('scale');
 74→@Cd = sin(foo);
 75→```
 76→Demonstrates the progression from integer division producing chunky results to proper float casting for smooth sine wave color gradients. Shows how integer math causes discrete stepping in the sine function output, and how casting @ptnum to float before division creates smooth continuous waves. Introduces channel references for interactive parameter control.
 77→
 78→### Sine waves with CH function [[Ep1, 48:34](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9gB1zBa9Lg4&t=2914s)]
 79→```vex
 80→@Cd = sin(float(@ptnum)/ch('scale'));
 81→
 82→// Evolution from hardcoded to parameterized:
 83→// @Cd = sin(@ptnum);
 84→// @Cd = sin(@ptnum/100);
 85→// @Cd = sin(float(@ptnum)/100);
 86→// @Cd = sin(float(@ptnum)/ch('scale'));
 87→
 88→// Can also assign to variable:
 89→float foo = float(@ptnum)/ch('scale');
 90→@Cd = sin(foo);
 91→```
 92→Creates a smooth sine wave pattern by dividing point number by a user-controlled scale parameter. The float cast prevents integer division truncation, allowing the sine function to produce smooth oscillations across points. Using ch('scale') enables interactive art direction of the wave frequency without editing code.
 93→
 94→## Trigonometric Functions
 95→
 96→### Clean Code and Using Variables [[Ep1, 55:52](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9gB1zBa9Lg4&t=3352s)]
 97→```vex
 98→float foo = @P.x/ch('scale');
 99→@Cd = sin(foo);
100→```
101→This example demonstrates clean code practices by using an intermediate variable 'foo' to store the scaled position calculation before applying the sine function to color. Using descriptive variable names and breaking calculations into steps makes code more readable and maintainable for yourself and others.
102→
103→### Length Function and Distance Calculations [[Ep1, 61:02](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9gB1zBa9Lg4&t=3662s)]
104→```vex
105→float d = length(@P);
106→@Cd = sin(d);
107→```
108→Demonstrates using the length() function to calculate the distance from the origin to each point's position, then applying sine wave modulation to create a color gradient based on that distance. This combines spatial distance calculation with trigonometric functions for visual effects.
109→
110→## Waves & Oscillation
111→
112→### Distance to Origin and Sine Wave [Needs Review] [[Ep1, 62:26](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9gB1zBa9Lg4&t=3746s)]
113→```vex
114→float d = length(@P);
115→@Cd = d;
116→
117→float d = length(@P);
118→@Cd = sin(d);
119→
120→float d = length(@P);
121→```
122→Calculates the distance from each point to the origin using length(@P) and applies it to the color attribute. The second variant applies a sine function to the distance value to create a wave pattern. The third snippet is incomplete but follows the same pattern of storing the distance calculation.
123→
124→## Trigonometric Functions
125→
126→### Distance and Sine Progression [[Ep1, 66:10](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9gB1zBa9Lg4&t=3970s)]
127→```vex
128→float foo = @P.y*ch('scale');
129→@Cd = sin(foo);
130→
131→float d = length(@P);
132→@Cd = d;
133→
134→float d = length(@P);
135→@Cd = sin(d);
136→
137→float d = length(@P);
138→d *= ch('scale');
139→@Cd = sin(d);
140→```
141→A progression showing how to calculate distance from origin using length(@P) and apply sine waves to create color patterns. Demonstrates building complexity by adding channel references to scale the distance values before applying sine function.
142→
143→### Scaling Distance with Compound Operators [[Ep1, 69:36](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9gB1zBa9Lg4&t=4176s)]
144→```vex
145→float d = length(@P);
146→d *= ch('scale');
147→@Cd = sin(d);
148→```
149→Demonstrates the *= compound assignment operator to scale the distance value by a channel slider. The compound operator d *= ch('scale') is shorthand for d = d * ch('scale'), allowing the distance to be multiplied and reassigned in one line. This scaled distance is then used in a sine function to create rings whose frequency is controlled by the scale parameter.
150→
151→### Scaling Distance with Compound Assignment [[Ep1, 69:40](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9gB1zBa9Lg4&t=4180s)]
152→```vex
153→float d = length(@P);
154→d *= ch("scale");
155→@Cd = sin(d);
156→```
157→Demonstrates using the compound assignment operator (*=) to scale the distance value by a channel parameter. This shorthand d *= ch("scale") is equivalent to d = d * ch("scale"), multiplying the existing distance variable by the scale parameter and reassigning it back to d. The scaled distance is then used in the sine function to create rings whose frequency can be controlled by the scale parameter.
158→
159→## Waves & Oscillation
160→
161→### Remapping sine wave range [[Ep1, 71:46](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9gB1zBa9Lg4&t=4306s)]
162→```vex
163→float d = length(@P);
164→d *= ch('scale');
165→@Cd = (sin(d) + 1) / 2;
166→```
167→Demonstrates remapping a sine function output from [-1, 1] to [0, 1] range for color values. First adds 1 to shift the range to [0, 2], then divides by 2 to compress it to [0, 1], ensuring valid color values. Uses parentheses to group the addition operation before division to achieve the correct order of operations.
168→
169→### Normalizing sine wave color range [[Ep1, 72:00](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9gB1zBa9Lg4&t=4320s)]
170→```vex
171→float d = length(@P);
172→d *= ch('scale');
173→@Cd = (sin(d)+1)/2;
174→
175→// Alternative compact form:
176→@Cd = (sin(length(@P) * ch('scale'))+1)*0.5;
177→```
178→Demonstrates how to normalize sine wave output from a range of [-1,1] to [0,1] for color values. The expression adds 1 to shift the range to [0,2], then divides by 2 (or multiplies by 0.5) to compress it to [0,1], preventing over-bright values that would clamp to white.
179→
180→## Trigonometric Functions
181→
182→### Vector scaling in distance calculations [[Ep1, 82:48](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9gB1zBa9Lg4&t=4968s)]
183→```vex
184→vector center = chv('center');
185→float d = distance(@P * {0.5,1,1}, center);
186→d *= ch('scale');
187→@Cd = (sin(d)+1)*0.5;
188→
189→// Alternative versions:
190→// float d = distance(@P, {1,0,1});
191→// d *= ch('scale');
192→// @Cd = (sin(d)+1)*0.5;
193→
194→// vector center = chv('center');
195→// float d = distance(@P, center);
196→// d *= ch('scale');
197→// @Cd = (sin(d)+1)*0.5;
198→```
199→Demonstrates multiplying the position vector by another vector to scale individual components before computing distance. Multiplying @P by {0.5,1,1} scales only the X component by 0.5, effectively stretching the distance calculation along the X-axis while leaving Y and Z unaffected.
200→
201→### Non-uniform position scaling for distance patterns [[Ep1, 83:36](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9gB1zBa9Lg4&t=5016s)]
202→```vex
203→vector center = chv('center');
204→vector pos = @P * {0.5, 1, 1};
205→float d = distance(pos, center);
206→d *= ch('scale');
207→@Cd = (sin(d) + 1) * 0.5;
208→```
209→Demonstrates non-uniform scaling of position components by multiplying @P with a vector constant, allowing independent control over pattern stretching along different axes. The X component is scaled by 0.5 while Y and Z remain at 1.0, creating an elliptical distance pattern instead of a circular one. This technique provides fine-grained control over pattern distortion before calculating distance to center.
210→
211→## Waves & Oscillation
212→
213→### Remapping sine waves with fit [[Ep1, 88:42](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9gB1zBa9Lg4&t=5322s)]
214→```vex
215→// Version 1: Manual remapping
216→vector pos = @P * chv('fancyscale');
217→vector center = chv('center');
218→float d = distance(pos, center);
219→d *= ch('scale');
220→@Cd = (sin(d)+1)*0.5;
221→
222→// Version 2: Using fit function
223→vector pos = @P * chv('fancyscale');
224→vector center = chv('center');
225→float d = distance(pos, center);
226→d *= ch('scale');
227→@Cd = fit(sin(d), -1, 1, 0, 1);
228→
229→// Version 3: Animating with @Time
230→vector pos = @P * chv('fancyscale');
231→vector center = chv('center');
232→float d = distance(pos, center);
233→d *= ch('scale');
234→@Cd = fit(sin(d * @Time), -1, 1, 0, 1);
235→```
236→Demonstrates three approaches to remapping sine wave output from [-1,1] to [0,1] for color visualization: manual calculation (sin(d)+1)*0.5, using the fit() function for clarity, and animating the pattern by multiplying distance by @Time before passing to sine. The fit() function provides a cleaner, more readable way to remap values between ranges.
237→
238→## Trigonometric Functions
239→
240→### Using fit() with sin() for animation [[Ep1, 94:18](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9gB1zBa9Lg4&t=5658s)]
241→```vex
242→vector pos = @P * chv('fancyscale');
243→vector center = chv('center');
244→float d = distance(pos, center);
245→d *= ch('scale');
246→@Cd = fit(d);
247→
248→vector pos = @P * chv('fancyscale');
249→vector center = chv('center');
250→float d = distance(pos, center);
251→d *= ch('scale');
252→@Cd = fit(sin(d), -1, 1, 0, 1);
253→
254→vector pos = @P * chv('fancyscale');
255→vector center = chv('center');
256→float d = distance(pos, center);
257→d *= ch('scale');
258→@Cd = fit(sin(d * @Time), -1, 1, 0, 1);
259→```
260→Demonstrates the progression of using fit() to remap values, starting with incomplete fit() calls, then properly using fit() to remap sin(d) from its natural range of -1 to 1 into the 0 to 1 range needed for color. Finally introduces animation by multiplying distance by @Time before passing to sin(), creating an animated ripple effect.
261→
262→### Animating with @Frame vs @Time [[Ep1, 97:12](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9gB1zBa9Lg4&t=5832s)]
263→```vex
264→vector pos = @P * chv('fancyscale');
265→vector center = chv('center');
266→float d = distance(pos, center);
267→@Cd = fit(sin(d*@Frame),-1,1,0,1);
268→```
269→Demonstrates animating color patterns using @Frame for rapid integer-based animation versus @Time for smoother float-based animation. The sine wave's frequency is modulated by distance from center and multiplied by the frame/time variable, creating animated ripple effects that evolve at different rates.
270→
271→### Code Style and Mathematical Operations [Needs Review] [[Ep1, 99:50](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9gB1zBa9Lg4&t=5990s)]
272→```vex
273→vector pos = @P * chv('fancyscale');
274→vector center = chv('center');
275→float d = distance(pos, center);
276→d *= ch('scale');
277→@Cd = fit(sin(d), -1, 1, 0, 1);
278→
279→foo -= 5;
280→foo *= 5;
281→foo -= foo * 5 / @P.x * @N.y;
282→
283→foo *= 1; // set range
284→foo -= 1; // make sure values never get below 0
285→foo /= @P.x; // reduce range to within red value
286→foo += @N.y; // addition normal on y
287→```
288→Demonstrates VEX code styling conventions and compound assignment operators for building complex mathematical expressions. Shows how to chain operations and use comments to document intent, building color patterns from distance calculations and normal attributes.
289→
290→### Length and Clamp Functions [[Ep2, 18:52](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OyjB5ZifIuU&t=1132s)]
291→```vex
292→float d = length(@P);
293→v@z = ch("_scale");
294→@P += @N * sin(d) * ch("wave_height");
295→```
296→Demonstrates using the length() function to calculate distance from origin, combined with clamp() for value constraining and sin() for wave deformation. The final example shows how to create a radial wave effect by offsetting points along their normals based on the sine of their distance from the origin, scaled by a channel parameter.
297→
298→### Distance normalization and clamping [Needs Review] [[Ep2, 27:28](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OyjB5ZifIuU&t=1648s)]
299→```vex
300→float d = length(@P);
301→@P.y = d*d + 1 * 10;
302→d = asin(clamp(d, 0, 1));
303→```
304→This code calculates the distance from the origin using length(), applies a squared transformation to the Y position, then uses asin() with a clamped distance value to ensure input values stay within the valid range of -1 to 1. The clamping prevents values from going outside the acceptable domain for arcsine, avoiding errors while preserving all geometry.
305→
306→## Waves & Oscillation
307→
308→### Animating Sine Waves with Ramps [[Ep2, 49:06](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OyjB5ZifIuU&t=2946s)]
309→```vex
310→float d = length(@P);
311→d *= ch('scale');
312→d += @Time;
313→d = sin(d);
314→d = fit(d,-1,1,0,1);
315→```
316→This example demonstrates animating a sine wave pattern by adding @Time to the distance calculation, creating ripple animation across a grid. The sine output is remapped from [-1,1] to [0,1] using fit(), making it suitable for driving a ramp parameter that controls the vertical displacement of points.
317→
318→### Animating Grid with Ramp and Sin Wave [Needs Review] [[Ep2, 49:12](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OyjB5ZifIuU&t=2952s)]
319→```vex
320→float d = length(@P);
321→d *= ch('scale');
322→d *= ch('width');
323→d = sin(d);
324→d = fit(d, -1, 1, 0, 1);
325→```
326→Creates an animated wave pattern by calculating distance from origin, scaling it with channel parameters, applying a sine function, and remapping the result from the sine range (-1 to 1) to (0 to 1). This technique generates ripple effects that can be further modulated with time.
327→
328→### Animating with sine wave [[Ep2, 49:14](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OyjB5ZifIuU&t=2954s)]
329→```vex
330→float d = length(@P);
331→d *= ch('scale');
332→d += @Frame;
333→d = sin(d);
334→d = fit(d, -1, 1, 0, 1);
335→```
336→Creates animated wave pattern by calculating distance from origin, adding frame number for time evolution, applying sine function, and remapping the result from [-1,1] to [0,1] range. This produces a rippling wave effect that animates over time.
337→
338→## Trigonometric Functions
339→
340→### Animating Height with Ramp and Sine [[Ep2, 49:40](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OyjB5ZifIuU&t=2980s)]
341→```vex
342→float d = length(@P);
343→d *= ch('scale');
344→d += @Frame;
345→d = sin(d);
346→d = fit(d,-1,1,0,1);
347→```
348→This code calculates distance from origin using length(@P), scales and offsets it by frame number to create animation, then applies sine wave and fits the result from [-1,1] to [0,1] range. The original version used chramp() to drive the Y position, but the final version demonstrates a mathematical approach using sin() and fit() for wave animation.
349→
350→### Animated Ripple with Nested Trigonometry [[Ep2, 52:12](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OyjB5ZifIuU&t=3132s)]
351→```vex
352→float d = length(@P);
353→d *= ch('scale');
354→d -= @Time;
355→@P.y = sin(d/cos(d/cos(d)));
356→@Cd = ch('height');
357→```
358→Creates an animated ripple effect using nested trigonometric functions where distance from origin is scaled, time-shifted, and passed through nested sine and cosine operations. The complex nesting of sin(d/cos(d/cos(d))) produces interesting wave interference patterns that evolve over time.
359→
360→## Waves & Oscillation
361→
362→### Sine Wave Pattern with Fit [[Ep2, 52:56](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OyjB5ZifIuU&t=3176s)]
363→```vex
364→float d = length(@P);
365→d *= ch('scale');
366→d += @Time;
367→d = sin(d);
368→d = fit(d, -1, 1, 0, 1);
369→```
370→Creates an animated radial sine wave pattern by calculating distance from origin, scaling and offsetting by time, applying sine function, then remapping the result from the sine range (-1 to 1) to a normalized 0-1 range. The fit() function normalizes the sine wave output for consistent attribute values that work better with color or other normalized attributes.
371→
372→### Sine Wave with Ramp and Fit [Needs Review] [[Ep2, 65:32](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OyjB5ZifIuU&t=3932s)]
373→```vex
374→float d = length(@P);
375→d *= ch('scale');
376→d += @Time;
377→d = sin(d);
378→d = fit(d, -1, 1, 0, 1);
379→@P.y = chramp('myRamp', d);
380→@Cd = ch('height');
381→```
382→Creates an animated sine wave pattern by calculating distance from origin, applying time offset, and using the result to drive both vertical position via a ramp lookup and color. The sine output is remapped from [-1,1] to [0,1] using fit() to work properly with chramp().
383→
384→## Trigonometric Functions
385→
386→### Normalizing Values with Fit [[Ep2, 66:46](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OyjB5ZifIuU&t=4006s)]
387→```vex
388→float d = length(@P);
389→d *= ch("scale");
390→d += @Time;
391→d = sin(d);
392→d = fit(d, -1, 1, 0, 1);
393→@Cd.y = chramp("my ramp", d);
394→@Cd.x = chf("height");
395→```
396→This code demonstrates normalizing sine wave values from their natural range of -1 to 1 into a 0 to 1 range using the fit function. The normalized value is then used to drive color through a ramp parameter and height channel, showing how normalized values are essential for controlling attributes like normals and directions versus scalar values like speed.
397→
398→## Waves & Oscillation
399→
400→### Sine Wave Output Range [[Ep2, 67:54](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OyjB5ZifIuU&t=4074s)]
401→```vex
402→@P.y = sin(@P.x);
403→```
404→The sine function outputs values constrained between -1 and 1, with the lowest point at -1 and highest at 1. When used with sin(@P.x), it creates a wave pattern where the height never exceeds these bounds, similar to but distinct from a fit01 operation.
405→
406→## Trigonometric Functions
407→
408→### Length function with fit remapping [[Ep2, 6:10](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OyjB5ZifIuU&t=370s)]
409→```vex
410→int i = 1 + len(@P);
411→f@x = fit(sin(i), 0, 1, 0.1, 3.1);
412→```
413→Uses the len() function to calculate the length of the position vector, adds 1, then applies sine and remaps the result from 0-1 range to 0.1-3.1 range using fit(). This creates a custom floating point attribute 'x' based on oscillating position vector magnitude.
414→
415→## Waves & Oscillation
416→
417→### Sine Wave Ripple with Color Ramp [[Ep2, 71:20](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OyjB5ZifIuU&t=4280s)]
418→```vex
419→float d = length(@P);
420→d *= ch('scale');
421→d = sin(d);
422→d = sin(d);
423→d *= fit(d, -1, 1, 0, 1);
424→@Cd = chramp('my_ramp', d);
425→@P.y *= ch('height');
426→```
427→Creates a radial sine wave pattern from the origin using point distance, applies double sine for more complex oscillation, remaps the result to 0-1 range, and colors points using a ramp while scaling vertical positions. The double sine operation creates a more intricate wave interference pattern.
428→
429→### Radial Sine Wave Color and Height [[Ep2, 71:30](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OyjB5ZifIuU&t=4290s)]
430→```vex
431→float d = length(@P);
432→d *= ch('scale');
433→d = sin(d);
434→d = fit(d,-1,1,0,1);
435→@Cd = set(d,d,d);
436→@P.y *= ch('height');
437→```
438→Creates a radial sine wave pattern by calculating distance from origin, applying sine function, then remapping the result to a 0-1 range for grayscale color assignment. The Y position is scaled by a channel parameter to control height variation.
439→
440→## Trigonometric Functions
441→
442→### Distance-Based Ripple Pattern [[Ep2, 74:16](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OyjB5ZifIuU&t=4456s)]
443→```vex
444→float d = length(@P);
445→d *= ch('scale');
446→d = sin(d);
447→d = fit(d, -1, 1, 0, 1);
448→@Cd = vector(d);
449→@P.y *= ch('height');
450→```
451→Creates a radial ripple pattern by calculating distance from origin, applying sine wave modulation, and remapping the result to color. The pattern is controlled by channel parameters for scale and height, demonstrating how to combine mathematical functions with UI parameters for procedural effects.
452→
453→### Distance-based Y displacement with channels [[Ep3, 13:50](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fOasE4T9BRY&t=830s)]
454→```vex
455→float d = length(@P);
456→d *= ch("scale");
457→@P.y = sin(d);
458→```
459→Calculates the distance from the origin for each point, scales it by a channel parameter, and uses the sine of that scaled distance to displace points vertically. This creates a ripple effect where the wave frequency is controlled by the scale channel parameter.
460→
461→## Waves & Oscillation
462→
463→### Sine Wave Amplitude and Distance Falloff [[Ep5, 64:18](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qPwiuQUT-N4&t=3858s)]
464→```vex
465→int pts[];
466→int pt;
467→vector pos;
468→float d, w;
469→
470→pts = nearpoints(1, @P, ch('radius'), chi('number_of_points'));
471→
472→pt = pts[0];
473→pos = point(1, 'P', pt);
474→d = distance(@P, pos);
475→w = d * ch('freq');
476→w = sin(w);
477→w = ch('amp') * w;
478→w *= fit(d, 0, ch('radius'), 1, 0);
479→@P.y += w;
480→```
481→Applies a sine function to the distance-based frequency value, then multiplies by an amplitude parameter to control wave height. Finally, multiplies the wave by a fit function that inverts the distance, creating a falloff effect where waves are stronger near the source point and fade toward the radius edge.
