644→Creates a rotating up vector using sine and cosine functions driven by @Time. The sine and cosine functions working in conjunction create circular motion by oscillating between 1 and -1, while keeping the middle component at zero ensures the vector rotates in a horizontal plane. This rotating up vector, combined with a normal pointing up, causes geometry (like cubes) to spin around the normal axis.
645→
646→### Animating Up Vector with Time Offsets [[Ep6, 47:48](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yPaWl3AiSrc&t=2868s)]
647→```vex
648→v@up = set(sin(@Time), 0, cos(@Time));
